Java 25 Features — Interview Focus ⭐⭐⭐

Java 25 is an LTS release, released on September 16, 2025.

For your Java + Spring Boot interviews, don't try to memorize all JEPs. Focus on the features below.

1. Module Import Declarations ⭐⭐⭐

Java 25 makes it possible to import all packages exported by a module.

Instead of many individual imports, you can use:

import module java.sql;

This is a permanent Java 25 feature (JEP 511).

Interview trick:

Module import = import all packages exported by a module.

2. Compact Source Files + Instance main() ⭐⭐⭐

This feature became permanent in Java 25.

Traditional Java:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}

Java 25 allows a much simpler style for small programs:

void main() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}

This is mainly intended to make Java easier for beginners and small programs.

Important: Don't confuse this with Java 21. It was first previewed in Java 21 and became permanent in Java 25.

3. Flexible Constructor Bodies ⭐⭐⭐

Java 25 makes Flexible Constructor Bodies permanent.

It allows statements to execute before the explicit constructor invocation in certain situations.

Conceptually:

class Employee extends Person {

    Employee(String name) {

        // validation / preparation
        if (name == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        }

        super(name);
    }
}

This gives more flexibility around constructor initialization. It is a permanent Java 25 feature (JEP 513).

4. Primitive Types in Pattern Matching ⭐⭐⭐

This is an important language enhancement, but it is still a preview feature in Java 25.

Java 25 extends pattern matching so primitive types can participate in instanceof, switch, and pattern contexts.

For example, the direction is toward code such as:

switch (value) {
    case int i -> System.out.println("Integer: " + i);
    case long l -> System.out.println("Long: " + l);
    default -> System.out.println("Other");
}

Interview note: Say "preview feature in Java 25", not a fully finalized feature.

5. Scoped Values ⭐⭐⭐⭐

This is particularly relevant when discussing Virtual Threads and modern concurrency.

Scoped Values provide a way to share immutable data with a method and its callees, including child threads. They are designed as a more efficient and easier-to-reason-about alternative to many ThreadLocal use cases, especially with virtual threads.

Conceptually:

Request
   ↓
Scoped Value
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Repository

All can access the scoped context without passing the value through every method.

Interview trick:

ThreadLocal → mutable thread-local context
ScopedValue → immutable scoped context

6. Structured Concurrency ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Structured Concurrency treats multiple related concurrent tasks as one unit of work.

For example:

Request
 ├── Call Customer Service
 ├── Call Account Service
 └── Call Transaction Service

Instead of managing these independently, structured concurrency allows them to be treated as one logical operation, simplifying cancellation, error handling, and observability.

Important: In Java 25, Structured Concurrency is still a preview feature.

This is very useful for your microservices/system-design interviews.

7. Stable Values ⭐⭐

Java 25 introduces Stable Values as a preview API.

The idea is to hold immutable data while allowing its initialization to happen later than a normal final field.

Conceptually:

final field
    ↓
value fixed during construction

Stable Value
    ↓
value can be initialized later
    ↓
then remains immutable

The JVM can treat stable values similarly to constants for optimization.

8. Compact Object Headers ⭐⭐⭐

This is a JVM performance improvement.

On 64-bit architectures, Java 25 reduces object headers to 64 bits in the relevant HotSpot implementation, helping reduce heap usage and improve data locality.

This is more relevant for:

JVM performance
memory optimization
high-scale applications
9. Ahead-of-Time Improvements ⭐⭐⭐

Java 25 improves AOT (Ahead-of-Time) behavior.

Two important enhancements are:

AOT Command-Line Ergonomics

Makes creating AOT caches simpler.

AOT Method Profiling

Previous method-execution profiles can be available when the JVM starts, helping the JIT compiler generate optimized code sooner.

Think:

Normal startup
    ↓
Application starts
    ↓
Collect profile
    ↓
JIT optimizes

Java 25 AOT improvements
    ↓
Previous profile available
    ↓
Optimization can happen earlier
    ↓
Faster startup
10. Key Derivation Function API ⭐⭐⭐

Java 25 adds a standard KDF API for cryptographic key derivation.

KDF means:

Key Derivation Function

It derives cryptographic keys from existing secret material and other inputs.

This is particularly relevant to modern cryptography and security.

11. PEM Encodings ⭐⭐

Java 25 adds a preview API for encoding and decoding cryptographic objects in PEM format.

PEM is commonly seen with:

-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
...
-----END CERTIFICATE-----

and cryptographic keys/certificates.

12. JFR Improvements ⭐⭐

Java Flight Recorder gets several improvements, including:

CPU-time profiling
Cooperative sampling
Method timing and tracing

These help with JVM performance analysis and troubleshooting.

🔥 Java 17 → 21 → 25

This is the easiest way to remember the progression:

Version	Major things to remember
Java 17	Records, Sealed Classes, Pattern Matching instanceof
Java 21	Virtual Threads, Record Patterns, Pattern Matching switch, Sequenced Collections
Java 25	Module Imports, Compact Source/Instance main, Flexible Constructor Bodies, Scoped Values, Structured Concurrency, AOT improvements

Java's language-change summary confirms the permanent Java 25 language additions are Module Import Declarations, Compact Source Files/Instance main, and Flexible Constructor Bodies.

🧠 Best interview memory trick
Java 8
→ Lambda + Stream + Optional

Java 11
→ String + HttpClient + Files

Java 17
→ Record + Sealed + instanceof Pattern

Java 21
→ Virtual Threads + Record Pattern + Switch Pattern

Java 25
→ Module Import + Simple Main + Flexible Constructor
→ Scoped Values + Structured Concurrency
→ AOT + Compact Object Headers
⭐ For your interviews, prioritize

Java 21:
Virtual Threads ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Java 25:
Scoped Values ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Structured Concurrency ⭐⭐⭐⭐
Flexible Constructor Bodies ⭐⭐⭐
Module Import ⭐⭐⭐
Compact Source Files / Instance main ⭐⭐⭐
AOT improvements ⭐⭐

One important distinction: Scoped Values and Structured Concurrency are not both final Java 25 features—Structured Concurrency remains preview, while Scoped Values is listed as a Java 25 API feature; so mention their status accurately if asked.